{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he held immense influence within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by constant warfare with Muslim forces. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab yet he eventually secured his release to govern his lands with strategic acumen while navigating complex political factions among the Crusader nobility. He often acted as a regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and this role placed Raymond at the center of royal decision making regarding military campaigns and diplomatic alliances.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond faced significant challenges during his youth since he was captured by Nur ad-Din Zengi and held prisoner for roughly ten years before paying a ransom to regain freedom. This long period of incarceration shaped his worldview and made him cautious about engaging in reckless military adventures that could endanger the fragile Crusader states. His experience taught him the value of negotiation over direct confrontation whenever possible and this approach defined much of his later political career.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count became regent for the young King Baldwin IV and he worked tirelessly to maintain stability within the kingdom despite internal divisions between the court party and the nobility. Raymond opposed the marriage of Queen Sibylla to Guy of Lusignan because he feared that Guy lacked the necessary wisdom to lead the realm effectively against Saladin. This disagreement created deep rifts within the Christian leadership and weakened their ability to present a unified front against external threats looming on the horizon.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nRaymond retired to Tripoli after failing to prevent Guy of Lusignan from ascending to power and he spent his final days trying to secure peace through treaties with Saladin rather than engaging in futile battles. His death occurred shortly before the catastrophic Battle of Hattin where the Crusader army suffered a devastating defeat that ultimately led to the loss of Jerusalem. Historians view Raymond as a pragmatic leader who understood the limitations of Crusader power and sought realistic solutions to ensure survival instead of pursuing glorious but dangerous military ideals. His life represents a critical chapter in Crusader history where diplomacy clashed with religious fervor and where sound judgment often lost out to ambitious folly. The absence of his steady hand during the final crisis contributed greatly to the collapse of the kingdom he tried so hard to protect from total annihilation.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -2.5034264e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.004363566}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.01980597}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.02773276}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -1.6689314e-06}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0007459919}, {"token": " held", "logprob": -1.6676073}, {"token": " immense", "logprob": -0.60231364}, {"token": " influence", "logprob": -0.019491212}, {"token": " within", "logprob": -0.110